import json, hashlib, re, collections
from pathlib import Path
from datetime import datetime, timezone
P=Path('batches/glossary_batch_aws_devops_engineer')
inputs=['coverage_briefs.json','extracted_concepts.json','depth_classification.json']
briefs=json.loads((P/inputs[0]).read_text())['briefs']
extracted=json.loads((P/inputs[1]).read_text())
concepts=extracted['concepts']
depths={(x['concept_id'],x['path_id']):x['depth_tier'] for x in json.loads((P/inputs[2]).read_text())['classifications']}
rows={}
def add(i, level, intro, example, contrast, exids=None, coids=None, note=''):
    rows[i]=dict(level=level,intro=intro,example=example,contrast=contrast,exids=exids,coids=coids,note=note)

add(0,'MEDIUM',
'''You are about to operate in a sandbox, but your workstation has more than one approved AWS context. Before issuing a resource-changing command, make the chosen context explicit. A profile name is a starting point for checking the caller, not a substitute for checking it.''',
'''Suppose the exercise supplies a profile named lab-operator and expects a particular account. Replace NAME with lab-operator in the identity command. Read both the caller ARN and account ID in the result and compare them with the supplied operator context. Then use the configuration-list command with that same profile. Read the effective Region and the origin of the values rather than inferring them from the profile's label. If the approved command targets a different Region, substitute that Region for REGION in its --region option. Keep the same explicit profile on the command. This example checks identity and effective configuration separately: knowing which account is selected does not answer which Region the command will use.''',
'''Do not treat an account check as a Region check. get-caller-identity inspects the caller ARN and account ID; configure list inspects effective configuration and its origins. --region overrides the configured Region for that command, not the meaning of the caller ARN.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Keep credential creation out of scope; use supplied approved operator credentials and require the external secrets-handling preparation.')
add(1,'HIGH',
'''A lab needs two subnets for workloads placed in different Availability Zones. Address allocation has two separate questions: do the ranges fit without overlap, and does each range leave enough usable addresses? Complete the external IP-addressing and CIDR preparation before this AWS-specific allocation.''',
'''Take a supplied VPC range of 10.20.0.0/16. Two candidate subnets are 10.20.1.0/24 and 10.20.2.0/24. They lie inside that VPC and do not intersect. A /24 contains 256 addresses; after the five AWS reservations, 251 remain usable. For 10.20.1.0/24, the reserved addresses are .0 through .3 and .255. Compare 251 with the supplied capacity requirement, not 256. Place the two separate subnets in the two intended Availability Zones. Now consider changing the second candidate to 10.20.1.128/25: that range lies inside the first subnet, so it fails the nonoverlap check even though it is inside the VPC. Containment and nonoverlap are distinct tests, and both must pass.''',
'''A larger VPC does not make overlapping subnet ranges valid. Nor does an address count before reservations establish usable capacity. Check the VPC boundary, intersections with other subnets, and usable addresses separately; a subnet also belongs wholly to one Availability Zone.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f3,f4', 'Do not reteach CIDR arithmetic; require demonstrated external networking.ip_addressing_and_cidr mastery.')
add(2,'LOW',
'''A team choosing a container platform asks who will operate each part of it. The useful comparison is not simply whether a service is managed. Identify which platform work the service handles and which workload work remains with the team.''',
'''Imagine an approved workload requirement that calls for Kubernetes APIs and its ecosystem. Managed EKS supplies the Kubernetes control plane, but the team still configures and operates Kubernetes workloads; worker-node responsibility depends on the selected compute option. Compare that requirement with an alternative that only needs managed container scheduling and can be operated through ECS. Record both the platform requirement and the remaining customer duties. The choice should reflect the team's willingness to operate those workloads, not just a preference for the word managed. This is an ownership comparison, not an exercise in administering a self-managed Kubernetes control plane.''',
'''Managed does not mean the application is operated for you. ECS customers supply and operate applications; EKS customers retain workload configuration responsibilities and may retain worker-node duties. Keep that compute-option qualification when describing EKS ownership.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2', 'Keep this comparison short; do not introduce self-managed Kubernetes administration.')
add(3,'HIGH',
'''A subnet's intended internet route has been added to a route table, yet the operator still needs to establish which table the subnet actually uses. Routing starts with association, then destination matching. Use the CIDR knowledge completed in external preparation.''',
'''Suppose the intended table contains a route for 10.20.0.0/16 and an internet-gateway route for 0.0.0.0/0. A destination of 10.20.2.10 matches both prefixes; the /16 is more specific, so the default route does not take priority. An example destination of 198.51.100.8 matches the default route when no more specific destination route applies. Before reasoning from those entries, inspect the subnet's association. If it has no explicit association, it uses the VPC main table, which may not be the table you edited. Associate the subnet with the intended table and check the default route's target is the attached internet gateway. The routing claim here is about the selected route, not a claim that every other requirement for a working connection has been met.''',
'''Adding 0.0.0.0/0 is not an instruction to override every other route. More specific matching destinations take priority. Also, editing a table is not proof that a subnet uses it: an explicit association or the main-table fallback determines that.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Teach route selection only; leave security rules and end-to-end connectivity diagnosis to their later concepts.')
add(4,'LOW',
'''A business owner says a service may be unavailable briefly, but only a small amount of recent data may be lost. Those are two requirements, not one general promise of fast recovery. Name the tolerated downtime and tolerated data loss separately.''',
'''For an illustrative requirement, suppose the owner accepts at most 30 minutes to restore service and at most 5 minutes of lost data. Record RTO as 30 minutes and RPO as 5 minutes. The first number answers how long service restoration may take after disruption; the second answers how much data loss, measured in time, is acceptable. A proposed recovery capability must be compared with both requirements. If its restoration time meets 30 minutes but its recoverable data is 20 minutes behind the disruption, it does not satisfy the stated data-loss objective. Conversely, sufficiently recent data does not by itself establish timely restoration. These numbers are example business requirements, not recommended AWS defaults.''',
'''RPO is not another name for recovery duration. RTO bounds the time to restore service; RPO bounds acceptable data loss measured in time. Derive both from the business requirement rather than choosing whichever number is easiest to meet.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Keep measurement and rehearsal procedures for the later recovery-verification concept.')
add(5,'LOW',
'''Two reports arrive in the operations queue: an EC2 operating-system update report and an RDS parameter-check report. To route the work correctly, look at what the service manages and what the customer still controls, rather than treating all managed services alike.''',
'''Label the EC2 report as customer guest-operating-system work, including updates and security patches. For the RDS report, distinguish AWS-managed database infrastructure and operating-system maintenance from the customer's data, application use and customer-controlled engine configuration. A parameter group contains database engine configuration values, so the supplied parameter-check report concerns customer-controlled configuration. In the handoff, name the service boundary alongside the report: guest OS for EC2, engine configuration values for this RDS check. That makes the assignment specific enough to act on without claiming the customer operates RDS infrastructure. Data and access responsibilities also remain part of the customer side of the boundary.''',
'''AWS-managed infrastructure is not a blanket transfer of every operational duty. In particular, EC2 guest-OS patching remains customer work, while RDS operating-system maintenance is AWS work. RDS customer configuration and data duties still remain.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f3,f4', 'Do not introduce database capacity selection or specialist database administration.')
add(6,'LOW',
'''One requirement asks for more throughput during busy periods. Another asks for service to continue after a component fails. Both involve resources, but they ask different questions. Separate the amount of capacity from the independence of the capacity that remains after a failure.''',
'''Suppose a lab's demand requirement says it must handle 200 requests per second, while its failure requirement says it must keep serving after one component is lost. Increasing the size of a single component may address demand, but evaluating the failure requirement calls for independent redundant capacity. Write two conclusions: whether total capacity accommodates the supplied demand, and whether remaining independent components can meet the supplied service requirement after the loss. Adding or removing resources helps match changing demand; redundant components and multiple locations can support availability during a component failure. Keep the two requirements visible when comparing proposals, rather than accepting a larger component as the answer to both.''',
'''More capacity in the same component is not the same as independent redundant capacity. For a component-failure constraint, evaluate the latter; for changing demand, evaluate whether the workload has enough capacity. One measure does not replace the other.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Defer specific scaling-policy and controlled-AZ-failure procedures.')
add(7,'LOW',
'''An operator has an unresolved event and needs to know who can take responsibility. A resource name alone is not enough for a handoff. The response process needs an accountable owner and a defined next responder when routine work cannot resolve the issue.''',
'''For a sandbox orders service, suppose the supplied ownership record names the application operations team for the service and a separate team for an associated process. Use those named responsibilities when preparing the event-response record. Document the condition that requires escalation and identify the recipient rather than writing only escalate if necessary. If the current operator reaches the stated unresolved-issue condition, the record should show whom to engage and why. This example does not invent a universal escalation time or chain; the responsible teams and conditions come from the established environment. The operational value is that a later responder can find responsibility for both the resource and the process without guessing.''',
'''An escalation path is more than a list of contacts. The response process must document when an unresolved issue moves on and to whom. Naming a team for each resource and process supplies the ownership context for that decision.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use established owners and escalation conditions; do not author an organization-wide incident policy.')
add(8,'HIGH',
'''An operator requests a particular AWS action on a particular resource. A policy that looks permissive in general may not allow that request. Evaluate the request against the statement's actual action, resource and any conditions, using the access-control preparation already completed.''',
'''Consider supplied statements for an example object read. One Allow covers the requested action and the object's resource scope only when its Condition tests are satisfied. First compare the requested action with Action, then the target with Resource, then the request values with each applicable condition. If these checks do not find an applicable allow, the result is implicit deny. Now suppose an applicable Deny also covers the request: that explicit deny overrides the allow. Record which statement applies and which request values support the result rather than saying the policy allows reads in general. This example evaluates the identity-policy rules in this brief; it is not yet a full diagnosis of every policy type that can constrain AWS access.''',
'''An Allow somewhere in a policy is not an allow for every request. Scope and conditions must match. An applicable explicit deny still wins over an allow, and no applicable allow leaves the request implicitly denied.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Do not introduce session policies, boundaries or organization policies before their allowed contexts.')
add(9,'HIGH',
'''A supplied delivery workflow needs a runner and an observable execution. Read the workflow as a description of when work begins, where each job runs and what each step does. Then use execution evidence to determine what actually happened.''',
'''In an illustrative repository, locate the supplied YAML under .github/workflows. Read on to identify its triggering event, then locate the intended job under jobs. Its runs-on value selects that job's runner. In steps, distinguish an action referenced with uses from a supplied command executed with run. After the expected event, open that workflow run and inspect the job and step logs. Suppose the logs show two steps succeeded and the third failed: record those actual outcomes, not just the three steps declared in YAML. Keep the same run in view while following the evidence so that a log from another revision is not mistaken for this execution. The exercise uses supplied actions and commands, not independent application build logic.''',
'''Declared steps and completed steps are different evidence. The YAML describes the job and runner; the workflow run's logs establish which steps ran and whether they succeeded or failed. A runner selection alone is not proof of successful execution.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Require external Git and pipeline-stage preparation; use supplied commands instead of introducing new shell syntax.')
add(10,'LOW',
'''A delivery report shows deployments are more frequent, but recovery evidence is also changing. Read throughput and instability together before proposing a conclusion about the delivery workflow.''',
'''Suppose one period has more deployments and shorter commit-to-production time, but a larger proportion of deployments need intervention. The first observations concern deployment frequency and change lead time; the latter concerns change fail rate. Add the failed-deployment recovery-time evidence to understand how long those failures take to recover from. Compare these measures over time for the same application context. Do not turn one improving measure into a ranking of individual operators.''',
'''Frequent delivery alone does not describe stability. Keep failure proportion and failed-deployment recovery time beside throughput measures, and interpret the combined evidence within the application's context.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Keep metric definitions and interpretation bounded; do not introduce unsupported performance benchmarks.')
add(11,'HIGH',
'''An event rule should select the intended operational events without selecting unrelated examples. Build the matching requirement from named event fields, then test the boundary with examples that should pass and examples that should not.''',
'''Suppose the lab supplies events with source and detail-type fields. The intended pattern accepts source value lab.orders and detail-type value OrderReady. Represent each acceptable value in an array under its field in the JSON pattern. Test an event carrying those values, then change detail-type to OrderCancelled in a second sample and test again. Also test a sample that varies a field omitted from the pattern: omitted fields are not required to match. Compare each test result with the intended requirement before using the pattern in a rule. The names here are invented sample data; the event bus and real operational event fields must come from the supplied exercise. This stage teaches selection, not target execution permissions.''',
'''A pattern is not a requirement that every field in the event be identical to a sample. Fields omitted from the pattern are not required to match. Positive and negative tests make the intended field-level selection explicit.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Keep target authorization and delivery retries for their later concepts.')
add(12,'HIGH',
'''The launch request for an EC2 lab instance is ready, but an operator must connect the approved choices with the resource that was actually created. Treat launch inputs, instance identity, lifecycle state and health evidence as distinct parts of the check.''',
'''Use the exercise's approved AMI, instance type, subnet and supplied IAM instance profile in the launch workflow. The AMI supplies the initial software image; the instance profile attaches its role so applications can use temporary role credentials. After launch, record the created instance ID and compare its settings with the supplied choices. Next inspect instance state and status checks. If the state is running but a status check reports a problem, retain both observations: lifecycle state and reported host or instance health answer different questions. Do not replace the health evidence with the fact that a launch request completed. This is operation of the approved instance, not guest-OS configuration or independent role-policy design.''',
'''An instance being in a running lifecycle state does not make status-check evidence redundant. Read state and status checks separately. Similarly, the instance profile attaches the role; it is not a separate set of embedded application access keys.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Require external compute preparation; defer detailed health diagnosis and Linux service configuration.')
add(13,'MEDIUM',
'''A supplied deployment file must be stored and retrieved from the intended S3 location. Name both the bucket and the object key before transferring it, and keep the operator profile and Region explicit in the operation.''',
'''For an illustrative transfer, let LOCAL_FILE be orders-release.zip and let KEY be releases/orders-release.zip in the approved bucket. Substitute the supplied bucket, profile and Region into the upload command. To download the same object, reverse the source and destination: the S3 reference becomes the source and the local file path becomes the destination. The object key identifies the object within its bucket; do not compare only the local filename when confirming which object was retrieved. Check the intended bucket location and caller access as part of the exercise. The command template teaches the two transfer directions without introducing an independent bucket-policy or object-recovery workflow.''',
'''The object key is scoped within a bucket. A familiar key string alone does not identify the intended storage location. Check the bucket's Region and the caller's access rather than assuming either from the file's name.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Require external object-storage preparation; leave versioning and retention to later concepts.')
add(14,'HIGH',
'''An orders workload reports a metric, but the operator must select the same series that was published. The metric name is only part of that identity. Namespace and the full dimension combination also matter.''',
'''Suppose the supplied metric is QueueDepth in namespace Lab/Orders with dimensions Environment=sandbox and Service=orders. Publish the supplied value with put-metric-data using that namespace, name and dimension combination. When querying, select that same combination. A second publication with Environment=staging and Service=orders identifies a distinct metric, even though its name is also QueueDepth. In the exercise record, write the namespace, name and dimensions beside the observed value. That makes the comparison about a particular series rather than a name shared by several series. These dimension values are illustrative workload labels; do not add new dimension combinations just to make the example more elaborate.''',
'''Querying by a familiar metric name is not enough to establish that you selected the intended series. Each distinct dimension combination identifies a distinct metric. Compare the published and queried combinations, not just the displayed metric name.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not introduce dimension-cost claims or alarm thresholds not present in the brief.')
add(15,'MEDIUM',
'''A supplied fleet program receives target IDs from configuration. Before it may change resources, its validator needs an approved scope and a target limit. This task configures and exercises that validator; it does not ask you to write a new validation algorithm.''',
'''Suppose the exercise approves two instance IDs and a maximum of two targets. Set FLEET_ALLOWED_INSTANCE_IDS to those IDs separated by commas and FLEET_MAX_TARGETS to the positive integer limit supplied for the task. Environment-variable values are strings the program reads as configuration. Run the supplied malformed-input case and a case containing an ID outside the approved list. Inspect the validator result and the supplied operation evidence to confirm rejection happened before a resource-changing call. Keep the approved IDs and limit visible alongside the test results. A label that sounds safe is not the check; the evidence is that the provided validator rejects malformed and out-of-scope input at the required boundary.''',
'''FLEET_ALLOWED_INSTANCE_IDS and FLEET_MAX_TARGETS are configuration names understood by the supplied validator, not Python language keywords. Setting them is not proof of enforcement: exercise the rejection cases before enabling resource changes.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use the supplied validator; do not author general-purpose Bash control flow or new Python parsing logic.')
add(16,'HIGH',
'''An approved CloudFormation template is ready to provision a new sandbox stack. Before execution, the operator needs a preview of the proposed resource actions and a clear separation between review and execution.''',
'''Create the supplied template's CREATE change set and wait until change-set creation completes. Inspect additions, modifications, removals and any replacement indicators against the approved scope. For an illustrative review, suppose the preview contains the expected EC2 addition but also a removal the task does not authorize. Record that discrepancy for the approval decision rather than treating the preview as automatic authorization. When the set is approved, execute that specific set to provision the resources. A preview describes proposed changes without performing them, so retain execution evidence separately from the review. Even a preview that matches the requested scope does not guarantee execution will succeed.''',
'''Creating a change set does not provision its resources. It previews changes without executing them. Likewise, a clean review is not a guarantee of execution success; inspect the preview before authorization and distinguish it from the later execution outcome.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use the supplied template and approval path; leave rollback diagnosis to its dedicated concept.')
add(17,'HIGH',
'''A supplied policy should deny covered requests that are not MFA authenticated. The key question is what the condition means for the request's temporary credentials, including requests where the condition key is absent.''',
'''Inspect the supplied Deny statement's scoped actions and its BoolIfExists test on aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent set to false. Compare two approved test cases: one using qualifying MFA-authenticated temporary credentials and one without MFA. The condition can block the latter, including a request where the key is absent, but only subject to the statement's action scope. Record the request context and result for each test. Do not describe the policy as covering every AWS action if its supplied scope covers only selected actions. The exercise is a comparison of allowed MFA-authenticated and denied non-MFA requests under the supplied policy, not a general redesign of human authentication.''',
'''An absent key is not automatically outside a BoolIfExists Deny set to false. Keep that behavior and the statement's scoped actions together when explaining the result. Avoid turning this condition into an unqualified claim about all credentials or all requests.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2', 'Keep the qualifying-temporary-credential limitation; apply only the supplied scoped MFA policy.')
add(18,'HIGH',
'''A caller needs to enter a role, and that role needs permissions for the resulting session's work. These are different questions. Read trust as the role-entry boundary before evaluating what the assumed session may do.''',
'''For an approved cross-account caller, inspect the role trust policy's Principal, sts:AssumeRole action and supplied conditions. Then inspect the caller's permission to call sts:AssumeRole on that role. Test the approved principal and the supplied unapproved principal and retain their outcomes. Once a session is assumed, its allowed work is governed by the role's permissions policy rather than by treating the trust policy as an operations allowlist. For example, permitting entry to an orders-operator role does not itself describe which orders-resource actions that session may perform. The example keeps role entry and session permissions separate without inventing additional trust conditions or granting broader access.''',
'''A trust policy answers who may assume a role, not what an assumed session may do. Cross-account assumption also needs caller-side AssumeRole permission. An approved principal in the trust policy alone does not complete that cross-account permission check.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f3,f4', 'Use approved principals and supplied conditions; avoid advanced federation design.')
add(19,'LOW',
'''A root-protection review is not a routine operating task performed with root. It checks that exceptional account authority is protected and that normal work uses non-root identities.''',
'''Take the supplied root-use and access-key evidence and compare each item with the protection checklist. Confirm MFA protection in the review, note whether root access keys exist, and distinguish those keys from the non-root credentials used for routine operations. Do not create root access keys for the exercise. Existing root access keys that are no longer needed should be deleted under the approved process. Record findings against the checklist rather than using root to demonstrate ordinary resource operations. The purpose of the example is to interpret protection evidence and use the appropriate routine identity, not to explore every root-only capability.''',
'''Broad account authority is not a reason to use root for convenience. Root should not be used for routine tasks. MFA protection and root-key hygiene belong in the protection review; ordinary operational testing belongs under non-root identities.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Review supplied evidence; do not introduce or perform unapproved root-only operations.')
add(20,'MEDIUM',
'''A resource setting changed during a known interval, and the operator needs evidence of the actor and operation. Start with the selected Region and management-event history rather than assuming the record covers every kind of application activity.''',
'''Suppose the supplied incident interval is 09:00 to 09:15 and identifies a resource name. Filter event history by the relevant attribute and time window, then inspect candidate events. For a matching change, read eventTime, eventName, userIdentity and requestParameters or resources. Record the actor, operation, target and time from those fields. If several nearby events exist, keep the target and operation comparison explicit rather than assigning the change to the closest timestamp alone. The example establishes attribution of an AWS management change; it does not establish the sequence of all application requests or supply application tracing.''',
'''CloudTrail event history is not all application activity. It exposes recent management events for the selected Region. Keep that scope in the attribution record and derive the actor and target from event evidence, not from a guessed owner.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not introduce X-Ray/request tracing or infer causation beyond the supplied change evidence.')
add(21,'MEDIUM',
'''A cost report needs to distinguish orders-workload spend from other resource spend. A resource label can support that grouping only when the relevant cost-allocation tag has been activated for billing.''',
'''Suppose the intended tag key is Workload and an approved value is orders. Activate that cost-allocation tag, then group Cost Explorer costs by it for the supplied period. Compare the orders category with spend not allocated to the intended category. If a resource lacks the relevant active tag, do not assign its spend to orders just because its name resembles the service name. Record the chosen period and tag grouping with the result. This is attribution of billed costs using resource-associated labels, not a new estimate of what the workload ought to cost. The example shows why activation and resource tagging both matter to the intended grouping.''',
'''A label on a resource is not by itself the billing grouping check. Cost-allocation tags categorize costs after activation. Resources without the relevant active tag can leave spend unallocated, so the intended workload category may not contain all expected spend.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Defer workload cost estimation; use observed Cost Explorer evidence only.')
add(22,'HIGH',
'''An application writes structured events to a supplied file, and operators need those named fields in CloudWatch Logs. Follow one known event from its file source to the intended log stream rather than checking only that a log group exists.''',
'''For an illustrative orders event, the supplied JSON contains a request ID, an outcome and a timestamp. Configure the supplied collector with the application file path, intended group and intended stream name. Then verify that this known event appears in that stream with its structured fields preserved. The group provides shared retention and access settings, while the stream sequences events from a source. Named JSON fields can be discovered by Logs Insights, so preserving them supports later queries. Record the event identity and arrival location as the collection evidence. Do not claim that merely creating the group proves the collector sent the intended application event.''',
'''A log group and a log stream are not interchangeable labels. The group shares settings across streams; the stream holds a sequence of events from a source. Successful setup should include a known-event arrival check in the intended stream.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2', 'Do not teach later query syntax here or redesign application logging.')
add(23,'HIGH',
'''A lab asks for one Linux service setting to change and supplies files for returning to the previous working configuration. Before activating anything, identify those rollback files and establish how the supplied checks will judge the change.''',
'''Imagine the approved change modifies a setting in the supplied service configuration. First identify and verify the supplied rollback files. Change only the supplied setting, then run the supplied syntax or configuration check before activation. For a valid change, use the service's supported reload or restart procedure. Inspect both service status and the supplied effective-setting check: the latter answers whether the intended setting took effect. If the change fails, restore and reactivate the saved configuration using the approved procedure. Keep the check results with the change record so that a restart request is not mistaken for a verified outcome. Linux filesystem, permission and service-operation preparation must already be complete; the supplied files do not replace that mastery.''',
'''Activation is not validation. A reload or restart request does not replace the syntax/configuration check before activation or the status and effective-setting checks afterwards. Returning to the saved configuration also requires reactivation, not just restoring a file.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied rollback files; do not teach backup creation, boot-startup policy or independent Bash authoring.')
add(24,'HIGH',
'''A release requirement allows only limited exposure to a new version and needs a way to return traffic to a known-good version. Compare delivery strategies by their replacement and traffic behavior, not by assuming one is always safest.''',
'''Consider three proposals for the same approved release. Rolling replaces portions progressively. Blue/green maintains separate old and new environments and switches traffic. Canary exposes the new version to a limited traffic share before a broader rollout. Compare the supplied exposure constraint, available parallel capacity and rollback requirement with each proposal. If the requirement calls for separate old and new environments, the blue/green description directly addresses that arrangement. If it calls for limited initial traffic exposure, examine the canary proposal against that limit. Record how traffic can return to the known-good version under the supplied strategy instead of declaring that the strategy name guarantees recovery. The comparison is about stated requirements, not a universal ranking.''',
'''Rolling and canary are not simply two names for the same exposure pattern. Rolling describes progressive workload replacement; canary describes limited traffic exposure before broader rollout. Blue/green additionally uses separate old and new environments and a traffic switch.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f3', 'Defer product-specific rollout procedures; keep examples tied to stated exposure, capacity and rollback requirements.')
add(25,'HIGH',
'''A host signal should appear in CloudWatch under the correct instance identity. Use the supplied agent configuration to connect the chosen collection settings with a specific observed metric.''',
'''Choose the supplied host metrics under metrics_collected and append the intended InstanceId dimension. Start the agent through amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl with fetch-config, the supplied configuration location and -s. Then locate the expected metric in CloudWatch and compare its instance dimension with the approved host. A metric from a different instance is not verification for this host, even if the metric name matches.''',
'''Starting the agent is not the same as verifying collection. Check the expected metric and instance-identifying dimension in CloudWatch; the configuration describes what should be sent, while the observed series checks the intended host.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied configuration and agent-control procedure; require external package and Linux service preparation.')
add(26,'HIGH',
'''An alarm should reflect a supplied sequence of metric samples, including gaps. To explain its state, separate the statistic and threshold from the size of the evaluation window and the number of breaches required within it.''',
'''Suppose the approved samples for three evaluated periods are above, below and above the supplied threshold. Set EvaluationPeriods to 3 and DatapointsToAlarm to 2 for this example, alongside the supplied metric, statistic, period and comparison operator. There are two breaching datapoints in that window, matching the stated count requirement. Now examine the supplied missing-sample case with its selected missing-data policy. Do not silently treat a gap as a real zero or a real breach: use breaching, notBreaching, ignore or missing as configured. For the all-missing case, missing yields insufficient data; ignore retains the current state. Compare the resulting state with the supplied cases and retain the settings with the sample evidence.''',
'''EvaluationPeriods is not the number of breaches required. It is the number of evaluated periods; DatapointsToAlarm supplies the required breach count. Also, missing is the default missing-data setting, not a promise that every isolated gap immediately produces INSUFFICIENT_DATA.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f2,f4', 'Use supplied signals and thresholds; do not introduce unsupported sparse-data evaluation rules.')
add(27,'MEDIUM',
'''An operator needs approved human access without saving long-lived IAM user keys. Use the supplied Identity Center assignment and sign-in workflow, then test the access represented by the assignment.''',
'''For a supplied operator group, select the approved AWS account and permission set in the assignment workflow. Sign in to that account and test the intended access. Configure a named SSO profile with aws configure sso, then establish its session with aws sso login --profile NAME using that profile's name. Keep the assigned account and permission set alongside the test result rather than treating a successful sign-in as the complete access check.''',
'''A permission set describes permissions made available through account assignments. The assignment and sign-in do not replace testing the intended access. Use the SSO session workflow rather than storing long-lived IAM user keys for this exercise.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied assignments; do not create a new enterprise identity architecture.')
add(28,'HIGH',
'''A sandbox service should accept only its approved connection traffic. Build the required allow rule from the protocol, port range and source, then test the connection and its response. Apply the external ports/firewall preparation before this AWS rule exercise.''',
'''Suppose the supplied service requires TCP port 443 from an approved source range. Add that inbound allow rule on the associated security group and remove unnecessary ingress. Test the required connection and its response. For an allowed tracked connection, the response is allowed regardless of rules in the response direction because security groups are stateful. Keep the test about traffic the security group actually evaluates: Amazon-provided DNS and instance metadata traffic are not filtered by security groups. If the approved source is a security-group ID, it does not import that group's rules into the destination group. Use the supplied source and destination roles rather than widening ingress to make an unexplained test pass.''',
'''Security groups contain allow rules, not explicit deny rules. Also, a referenced source group does not contribute its own rule list to the destination group. Do not generalize the connection tests to traffic that security groups do not filter.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4,m1','f1,f4,m1', 'Keep DNS/IMDS filtering exceptions explicit; do not imply security groups filter every AWS-service flow.')
add(29,'HIGH',
'''A supplied EC2 instance specification must become a Terraform declaration with an approved provider version. Keep the provider selection, resource settings and execution review visible as separate parts of provisioning.''',
'''Replace APPROVED_VERSION in the provider declaration with the exact approved version. In the supplied aws_instance resource, use the supplied AMI, instance type and subnet ID; NAME is the declaration's chosen resource label. Run terraform init for provider installation, then terraform plan to inspect the intended instance changes. Compare those planned settings with the supplied specification before the approved apply step. After creation, inspect the live instance settings rather than accepting a syntactically plausible declaration as completion. This example teaches the bounded resource declaration and provider pin; it does not ask for an independent infrastructure design or a different provider version. The native EC2 foundation must be complete before interpreting these instance settings.''',
'''A provider pin and an instance declaration are not execution evidence. Initialization installs providers, planning inspects intended changes, and the approved apply creates the instance. Verification still needs the resulting instance settings.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Treat the HCL as a substitution template, not runnable with APPROVED_VERSION; do not introduce modules or backend configuration yet.')
add(30,'HIGH',
'''A caller's request reaches an S3 resource with a bucket policy. Evaluate the resource-side statement against that particular caller and target rather than treating an identity-policy allow as the whole resource-permission explanation.''',
'''Take a supplied object request and compare its caller with Principal, its operation with Action, its object ARN with Resource and its request values with Condition. A bucket ARN and an object ARN identify different scopes, so a statement about the bucket is not automatically a statement about that object request. For a same-account example, identity and resource permissions can contribute allows, but an applicable explicit deny overrides them. For the supplied cross-account example, keep the qualification that access generally requires permission on both sides. Record the matching scope and applicable deny evidence without turning that general cross-account rule into an exhaustive account of every special case.''',
'''A resource policy is attached to the resource and includes principal scope; it is not simply an identity policy moved to another file. Also, an applicable explicit deny still overrides contributed allows. Keep bucket and object ARN scopes distinct.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Preserve generally for cross-account requirements; avoid advanced grant exceptions not in this brief.')
add(31,'HIGH',
'''An account-onboarding request provides approved inputs and baseline checkpoints. Completing account creation is only part of the governed onboarding task; enrollment, placement and controls need evidence too.''',
'''Execute the established Account Factory workflow with the supplied account settings. Inspect the new account's enrollment and organizational-unit placement, then compare the applied controls with each supplied baseline checkpoint. If creation completed but a checkpoint has no supporting result, keep that distinction in the record. The account exists, but governance completion has not yet been demonstrated by the stated checks.''',
'''A created account is not automatically proof of completed governance. Verify enrollment, intended organizational-unit placement and supplied baseline checkpoints against the account's actual applied controls.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Execute the established workflow only; do not design a landing zone or invent baseline controls.')
add(32,'MEDIUM',
'''A supplied Python automation script has a requirements file. Before running it against AWS, prepare an isolated dependency environment and confirm the intended operator context.''',
'''Use python -m venv ENV_DIR with the supplied directory name, then activate it with the command for your platform; source ENV_DIR/bin/activate is the Unix example. Inside that environment, install requirements.txt with the stated pip command and inspect the package list. Run the supplied script with that environment's interpreter only after confirming the approved AWS profile and Region through its credential configuration. Record the environment and context used alongside the script outcome.''',
'''Dependency isolation and AWS target selection are separate checks. A virtual environment isolates interpreter context and installed packages; it does not replace confirming the approved AWS profile and Region.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f4', 'Require external Python and shell preparation; activation paths are platform-specific templates.')
add(33,'MEDIUM',
'''An inventory task needs all results in one approved account and Region. Page controls affect how results are retrieved or bounded, so read them before claiming that a listing is complete.''',
'''Run the supplied supported inventory command with explicit profile and Region and JSON output. For the full listing, keep automatic pagination enabled rather than adding --no-paginate. If the exercise deliberately uses --max-items to collect a bounded portion, retain the returned NextToken and resume with --starting-token for the remainder. Changing --page-size adjusts service-call page size; it is not the same as limiting total output with --max-items. Keep the continuation evidence with the inventory.''',
'''A smaller page size does not mean a smaller total inventory. Distinguish service-call page size from the output limit, and do not discard a continuation token when manually collecting the remainder.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Apply pagination only to supported commands; do not invent an inventory command or API continuation format.')
add(34,'LOW',
'''Another operator must perform a known task without relying on your memory. A useful runbook makes the conditions for acting and the evidence for advancing explicit.''',
'''For a supplied restart task, write its preconditions, approved ordered actions and expected results from the established procedure. Include the condition that stops routine work and name the escalation recipient. Read the draft as the receiving operator: can each expected result be checked before advancing? Replace an instruction such as confirm it worked with the supplied observable result. Do not invent a recovery action outside the approved procedure.''',
'''A list of actions alone is not an actionable runbook. Preconditions, expected results and failure/escalation instructions are needed so the operator can check execution rather than infer success.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use established procedures and owners; defer live incident execution to the later runbook-recovery concept.')
add(35,'HIGH',
'''A supplied EKS workload has a Pod that appears to be running. The operator still needs evidence about its containers and events. Pod phase is one lifecycle observation, not a complete health diagnosis.''',
'''Using the supplied cluster access setup, substitute the approved Pod name into kubectl get pod NAME. Record its phase, then inspect kubectl describe pod NAME for scheduling or runtime events. Use kubectl logs NAME -c CONTAINER for the intended container's output. Suppose the phase reads Running while the supplied events and logs show a container problem. Preserve those observations together rather than deciding that Running settles health. A Pod groups containers with shared networking and storage; its lifecycle is distinct from a controller that may replace it. This exercise inspects the Pod and its container output, not the controller's desired-replica reconciliation or cluster administration.''',
'''Running is a Pod phase, not a complete diagnosis of container health. Other phases include Pending, Succeeded, Failed and Unknown, but the phase list does not replace inspection of events and the intended container's logs.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied cluster access; leave Deployment reconciliation and self-managed cluster administration out of scope.')
add(36,'MEDIUM',
'''A managed database is available for an approved connection test. Verify the intended instance and the data marker separately; a resource status does not answer whether the expected application data is present.''',
'''Locate the supplied DB instance identifier in the console or describe-db-instances result. Compare its engine and operational status with the exercise requirements before using the supplied endpoint, client and approved credentials. Run the supplied query and retain its data-marker result. If the status is acceptable but the query does not return the expected marker, report that distinction rather than calling the data check successful. Record the instance identity and endpoint with the safe query outcome so the test can be tied to the intended database. This is bounded database operation with a supplied query, not a lesson in authoring SQL or selecting database capacity.''',
'''An acceptable instance status does not verify application data. The instance inspection checks identity, engine and status; the supplied connection and query check the data marker. Neither result should be used as a substitute for the other.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied client and query; do not teach SQL syntax or database capacity selection.')
add(37,'HIGH',
'''An S3 object was overwritten or simply deleted, and the lab requires an earlier version to remain recoverable. Versioning, restoration method and lifecycle expiry all affect the available recovery path.''',
'''Suppose the approved bucket contains two versions of releases/orders.zip and the newer one overwrote the earlier content. Select the required prior version and copy it to the same key; that creates a new current version with the selected content. For a separate simple-deletion case, inspect the delete marker and remove that marker to expose the preceding version. These are different recovery operations, so identify the actual case before acting. Inspect noncurrent-version expiration and other supplied lifecycle rules against the required recovery window. A retained prior version is useful only while it remains available under those controls. Do not delete a specified needed version: that operation permanently removes that version.''',
'''A simple delete in a versioning-enabled bucket ordinarily creates a delete marker; it is not equivalent to permanently deleting a specified version. Keep ordinarily in the explanation, and check lifecycle rules rather than assuming versioning alone preserves every version indefinitely.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f2,f4', 'Use approved versions and recovery window; distinguish marker removal from copying a prior version.')
add(38,'HIGH',
'''An already-built container image must reach an approved repository and then be retrieved as the exact published content. Keep the publication reference and the content digest separate in the evidence.''',
'''Substitute the supplied Region and registry in the authentication pipeline from get-login-password to docker login. Tag the already-built image with the approved REGISTRY/REPOSITORY:TAG and push that reference. Record the published image digest, then use the supplied digest in docker pull REGISTRY/REPOSITORY@sha256:DIGEST for the consumer test. Verify publisher and consumer access with their respective approved identities rather than using one identity for both roles. The digest identifies image content, while the repository and tag provide the publication reference in this exercise. The task begins with a built image and does not ask for Dockerfile authoring or a new container build process.''',
'''Publication is not the same as verified consumption. Push with the approved publisher, then retrieve the exact digest under the approved consumer and check its access. Do not substitute a convenient tag for the stated exact-content retrieval test.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Require external Docker and shell-pipe preparation; commands are supplied-value templates.')
add(39,'HIGH',
'''A release needs more than a downloadable build file: the operator must connect the exact content with its source and build provenance. Follow one supplied output through attestation, immutable publication, download and provenance verification.''',
'''Suppose the supplied output is an orders release file for revision rev-42. Use that file as subject-path for actions/attest@v4 with the stated workflow permissions. Publish the same file through actions/upload-artifact@v4 using its path and the supplied revision-based name. Retain the workflow run, artifact name and upload SHA256 digest as the content reference. Download it through actions/download-artifact and inspect any digest-mismatch warning; that action checks the upload digest. Then run the stated gh attestation verify template on the extracted output file for the approved repository. Inspect the verified JSON attestation for source revision and build workflow/run before deployment. The example keeps download-integrity evidence and provenance evidence connected to the same output rather than treating either one alone as the entire traceability record.''',
'''A digest check and provenance verification answer different questions. Download checks the upload SHA256 digest and warns on mismatch; the verified attestation provides source and build context. Do not claim the download action itself blocks every mismatch or proves the intended source revision.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use actions/attest@v4, not a substituted action; preserve the warning behavior and verify the extracted output file.')
add(40,'HIGH',
'''A supplied CloudFormation S3 template needs one approved property change. Read the declaration and references first, then verify that the live resource reflects the approved template rather than stopping at template validation.''',
'''Find the bucket's logical ID in Resources and inspect its Type and Properties. Type: AWS::S3::Bucket declares the bucket, and Ref on that logical ID returns its bucket name. Modify only the supplied property and references, then substitute the template file path in validate-template. Create the approved sandbox stack and inspect stack events and outputs. Finally, check the bucket's live value for the changed property. Keep declaration, deployment evidence and live-property evidence together: a correct-looking template is the requested state, while the live inspection checks what was deployed. This exercise uses the supplied standard template rather than introducing a new infrastructure language or unrelated resources.''',
'''Template validation is not verification of the bucket's live property. Inspect stack events, outputs and the deployed property after creating the sandbox stack. Also, Ref on an S3 bucket logical ID returns the bucket name, not an invented object reference.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f2,f3,f4', 'Use the supplied property; do not introduce new resource types or template functions.')
add(41,'MEDIUM',
'''A runtime consumer needs a managed secret, but the operator must verify use without revealing the value. Check the consumer's safe result rather than displaying the secret.''',
'''Use GetSecretValue with the supplied secret identifier under the approved runtime identity with secretsmanager:GetSecretValue permission. Feed the returned value to the supplied consumer and inspect its safe success result. Keep the secret out of source code and logs. The example verifies authorized runtime retrieval and use; it does not require copying the value into a debugging transcript.''',
'''Displaying a secret is not necessary evidence of successful retrieval. Use the consumer's safe result to verify success and do not write the value into source code or logs.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Do not teach managed-secret rotation; do not show example secret values.')
add(42,'LOW',
'''A team has several complaints about delivery, but the exercise asks for one evidence-backed improvement. Select a bottleneck supported by the supplied record and define a measurable result before proposing the change.''',
'''Suppose supplied delivery records show that a particular review handoff is the measured bottleneck. Propose one bounded change to that handoff and state the success signal the evidence will compare. Record the baseline for that signal, apply the approved change, then compare the after-change signal with the baseline. If the chosen measure does not improve, do not call the intervention successful merely because the change was completed. Keep the conclusion about this bottleneck and measured outcome, not about every aspect of delivery. The example does not assume a particular improvement always works; feedback and measured results determine whether it helped.''',
'''Implementing a change is not proof of improvement. Identify the evidence-supported bottleneck, state the expected measurable outcome and compare its success signal before and after. A general preference for automation is not the missing measurement.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied delivery evidence; avoid inventing performance targets or attributing results to individuals.')
add(43,'MEDIUM',
'''A Config rule reports a noncompliant resource. Before choosing a response, establish which recorded configuration differs from the rule's desired conditions and what the governing policy permits.''',
'''Open the supplied rule's noncompliant-resource result and record the resource identity. Compare the evaluation result and rule parameters with the affected resource's recorded configuration. Suppose the mismatch is a supplied setting outside the rule's required value; describe that setting mismatch rather than labeling the resource attacked. Choose the established remediation or escalation path from the governing policy. Preserve the evaluation evidence with that choice so the next operator can see why the response was selected. This is basic configuration-finding triage, not proof of malicious activity or permission to apply an unapproved change.''',
'''A compliance result reports configuration against desired conditions; it is not proof of an attack. Read the resource, evaluation and parameters before choosing the policy-governed remediation or escalation path.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not infer compromise or design specialist security remediation.')
add(44,'HIGH',
'''A stack's bucket property was changed outside CloudFormation in the controlled lab. Drift detection can show a declared-versus-current difference, but the operator must understand what it checked and what it did not do.''',
'''Run drift detection on the provisioned stack after the supplied out-of-band change and wait for completion. Locate the bucket's result and compare its expected and actual property values. Write the difference as a pair, such as supplied declared value A and current value B, rather than saying only drifted. Detection compares supported explicitly declared properties; unsupported or undeclared properties may not be checked. Keep that scope beside the result so a lack of reported drift is not described as exhaustive verification of every property. This task observes and records the difference. It does not repair the resource merely by running detection.''',
'''Drift detection reports differences without repairing them. It also does not promise to check every property: supported, explicitly declared properties are the comparison scope, and unsupported or undeclared properties may not be checked.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Keep detection distinct from repair; do not extend drift coverage beyond supported declared properties.')
add(45,'HIGH',
'''A controlled stack update failed. The response needs both the original failure evidence and the rollback outcome; a final stack label alone can hide the sequence that explains the problem.''',
'''Inspect the supplied update's stack events, starting with the first failing resource event and its failure reason. Then follow subsequent rollback events. If the resulting status is UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE, describe rollback to the previous working stack state. If it is UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED, the rollback failure must be resolved before continuing. Retain the first failure and the rollback status separately in the record so that the update problem is not confused with a later rollback problem. Use the supplied controlled failure and approved response path rather than experimenting with new changes while the rollback outcome is unresolved.''',
'''UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED is not another success label for rollback. It requires resolving the rollback failure before continuing. Conversely, UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE describes rollback to the prior working stack state, not successful deployment of the failed update.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Do not invent failure-recovery commands; inspect supplied events and established resolution guidance.')
add(46,'MEDIUM',
'''An EC2 application needs the approved role rather than embedded access keys. Attach the role through its instance profile, then test the resulting identity and bounded permissions from the instance.''',
'''Use the instance's IAM-role setting or the supplied associate-iam-instance-profile operation to attach or replace the approved profile. The profile contains one IAM role. From the instance, use the role credential provider, inspect caller identity and test one supplied allowed operation and one denied operation. Record the assigned role context with those outcomes. Merely seeing an attached profile is not the full permission test for the application.''',
'''An instance profile is the container used to pass one role to EC2, not a reason to embed keys. Test application operations under the role credential provider and inspect caller identity rather than assuming the attachment proves the intended access.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use the approved role and supplied test operations; do not design unrelated policies.')
add(47,'HIGH',
'''A supplied EKS workload needs AWS access under a particular role. Tie the association to the intended cluster, namespace and service account, then test both the associated and unassociated workload cases.''',
'''Create the approved Pod Identity association using the supplied cluster, namespace, service account and role. Run the workload with that serviceAccountName. The Pod Identity agent and supported SDK credential chain supply temporary role credentials to associated Pods. Verify the intended workload's allowed AWS access and the supplied unassociated workload's rejection. Keep the workload identity and association scope beside the results so access observed from one Pod is not assigned to another.''',
'''A role's existence is not the workload association. The association names a service account in a namespace and cluster, and the workload must use that serviceAccountName. Preserve the agent and supported-SDK qualifications when describing credential delivery.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied agent/access setup; do not introduce a different workload-identity mechanism.')
add(48,'HIGH',
'''A GitHub Actions workflow should enter an approved AWS role without stored AWS access keys. The trust boundary must identify the approved token context rather than accepting any workflow that can request a token.''',
'''Configure the supplied GitHub token issuer as the IAM OIDC provider. In the role trust policy, compare aud and sub with the approved audience and repository/branch or environment. Give the workflow id-token: write permission and configure the supplied AWS credential action to assume that role. Then run the approved case and the supplied unapproved repository or branch case. Verify the latter cannot assume the role. This test makes the trust scope observable rather than treating temporary credentials alone as evidence that only the intended workflow can get them.''',
'''Avoiding stored keys does not remove the trust-policy check. Constrain aud and sub to the approved context and test an unapproved repository or branch. A workflow's id-token permission alone does not establish the intended AWS role trust boundary.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Keep this GitHub OIDC mechanism separate from CodePipeline service-role trust.')
add(49,'HIGH',
'''An operator assumes an approved role for a bounded session. The returned credentials have three fields and an expiry, and the permissions explanation must keep role-policy grants distinct from resource-based grants.''',
'''Substitute the approved role ARN, session name and duration in the assume-role command. Use the returned access key ID, secret access key and session token together, and inspect Expiration. If a session policy is supplied, role identity-policy permissions are intersected with it. Do not extend that sentence to every resource grant: resource-based grants are evaluated separately, and grants directly to the session ARN are not limited by session policies. An applicable explicit deny still overrides an allow. Record the issued lifetime and the supplied permission evidence without displaying credential values. This example teaches session composition and the stated grant distinctions, not a new policy intended to exercise every possible permission case.''',
'''Temporary credentials are not just an access-key pair: the session token is also required, and the issued lifetime ends. Likewise, session-policy intersection describes role identity-based grants; it is not an unqualified limit on all resource-based grants.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not expose credential values; preserve direct-session-ARN and explicit-deny qualifications.')
add(50,'HIGH',
'''An event pattern matches the right event, but that does not authorize the target operation. Determine the target's required permission model before assigning execution authority.''',
'''For a supplied target that requires an execution role, grant events.amazonaws.com the approved trust to assume it and scope role permissions to the approved operation and resource. For a supplied SNS or Lambda target, use the required resource-based invocation permissions instead. Compare the selected target with its authorization model in the record. The selection pattern determines which events match; the separate authority determines whether the intended target operation is permitted. Do not assign a role to every target just because a different target used one.''',
'''Event selection and execution permission are separate. SNS and Lambda use resource-based invocation permissions in this brief, so assuming every target uses an execution role misstates the authorization model.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied targets and least-privilege permissions; do not invent target operations.')
add(51,'HIGH',
'''A managed Config rule is present, but the operator still needs to verify its scope, parameters and actual evaluation results. Rule existence is setup evidence, not the compliance result for every resource.''',
'''Choose the supplied rule and configure its approved resource scope and parameters. Run or inspect evaluation and compare the supplied compliant and noncompliant sample resources. For each sample, record the resource ID and compliance result, then relate that result to the rule criteria. If a noncompliant sample is still reported as compliant, inspect the chosen scope and parameters against the supplied requirement rather than accepting the presence of the rule as success. This example checks automated configuration evaluation; it does not install a remediation workflow or assume that a compliance report establishes malicious activity.''',
'''A configured rule does not mean all resources passed. Read individual evaluation resource IDs and compliance results. The rule evaluates supported configuration against its criteria and parameters; the actual results must be checked.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not add remediation behavior to a detection-only rule task.')
add(52,'MEDIUM',
'''An approved cost threshold should alert a named recipient. Configure the budget scope and notification deliberately, and do not describe an alert as an automatic resource stop.''',
'''Use the approved period, amount and scope for the cost budget. Choose the supplied actual or forecast threshold and approved notification recipient. Compare the configuration with those supplied inputs and retain the recipient setting. The budget compares cost or usage with an amount over a period; the notification alerts recipients. Any resource-stopping action would need separate configuration and is not part of this notification example.''',
'''A budget notification does not itself stop resources. It alerts recipients; separately configured actions are required for stopping behavior. Do not imply that notification setup caps the workload's spending.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Use supplied amounts and thresholds; do not estimate workload cost or configure unrequested budget actions.')
add(53,'HIGH',
'''A supplied application's commands produce an output that CodeBuild must export. Read the buildspec as both a phase-command plan and an artifact-selection plan, then compare it with the build evidence.''',
'''Use version: 0.2 in the supplied YAML buildspec. Place the supplied commands in commands lists under the needed phases, such as install, pre_build, build and post_build. Version 0.2 supports commands in the same shell environment. Set artifacts.files, and base-directory if needed, to match the generated output. In the resulting build, inspect each phase's logs and the exported artifact. Suppose the commands succeed but the selected artifact path does not match the generated file: that result does not verify the intended output was exported. Keep command execution and artifact selection visible together rather than treating a build completion label as the entire delivery check.''',
'''Command success and correct artifact export are not interchangeable. The buildspec describes both, and the task verifies phase logs and the exported output. Do not add unfamiliar build commands simply to fill every possible phase.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied commands and application output; require shared shell/testing preparation.')
add(54,'HIGH',
'''An approved EC2 application revision has deployment file mappings and lifecycle hooks. Connect the deployment group and AppSpec to the intended targets, then inspect what happened on those targets.''',
'''Inspect the supplied appspec.yml files mapping and listed lifecycle-hook scripts. Confirm the approved application, deployment group, intended EC2 targets, revision, service role and installed agent. Execute the supplied deployment action and inspect hook results. Then verify application files on the target against the expected mapping. If the action was requested but a hook failed or files are not as expected, retain that evidence rather than declaring deployment complete. This exercise uses supplied hook scripts and target setup; it does not ask for independent Bash authoring or a new application installation strategy.''',
'''A deployment-action request is not the target verification. AppSpec maps files and lists hooks, while hook results and target files show whether the supplied revision was applied as expected. The approved role and installed agent are part of the supplied setup.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not invent lifecycle ordering or hook script behavior beyond the supplied AppSpec facts.')
add(55,'HIGH',
'''A bounded load test should demonstrate EC2 Auto Scaling behavior within an approved group's limits. Keep the target metric, warmup and capacity settings beside the observed scaling activity.''',
'''Select the approved metric and target value for target tracking. Configure the supplied warmup and minimum, desired and maximum group capacities. During the bounded test, compare the metric values, scaling activities and resulting capacity. If capacity reaches the configured maximum, do not expect the group to exceed it merely because the metric is still above target. The evidence checks how this policy and these bounds behaved in the approved test.''',
'''Target tracking does not promise unlimited capacity. It adjusts capacity toward the metric target within the configured group limits. Read scaling activity and resulting capacity, not just the policy's existence.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Cover EC2 Auto Scaling only; defer load-driven ECS scaling and serverless/database capacity choices.')
add(56,'MEDIUM',
'''An ECR repository needs separate approved publisher and consumer access. Check repository operations and the identity-side authentication requirement rather than assuming one policy covers both.''',
'''Scope the supplied repository policy to approved principals and push/pull operations. Test push under the publisher, pull under the consumer and the supplied unapproved-principal case. Also inspect ecr:GetAuthorizationToken in the identity policy, because repository permissions alone do not grant that authentication action. Keep the policy scopes and test identities with the observed results.''',
'''Repository permissions alone do not grant ecr:GetAuthorizationToken. Authentication requires that action through an identity policy, while the repository policy covers the specified repository operations.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f3', 'Use supplied publisher/consumer roles; do not broaden repository scope to fix an unexplained denial.')
add(57,'HIGH',
'''Repository cleanup could remove an image version still needed for deployment or rollback. Preview the supplied ECR lifecycle policy against exact digests before applying it.''',
'''Express the approved retention selection with rulePriority, selection and action fields. Run the lifecycle preview and compare every previewed digest with the supplied deployed-version record. If a still-needed digest is eligible for expiry, exclude that needed version from the eligible selection before applying the approved policy. Keep the preview evidence with the retention decision rather than assuming a deployment reference protects the image.''',
'''Deployment does not make an image immune to lifecycle rules. Check previewed digests against needed versions; count, age and tag-status selection can still make an image eligible for expiry.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Do not invent lifecycle-policy JSON or retention durations; use supplied policy criteria.')
add(58,'HIGH',
'''An Application Load Balancer endpoint must route requests to the intended backend and judge target health with the intended check. Connect the listener, forwarding rule, target settings and health evidence before concluding that the path works.''',
'''Configure the supplied listener protocol and port, its forwarding rule, and the registered target protocol and port. Test the endpoint. If the path fails, compare the health-check path, port and success codes with the supplied backend requirement and inspect target health reasons. Suppose the health-check path is not the one the backend expects; use that evidence for the approved correction rather than changing unrelated settings. Keep the all-unhealthy case explicit: an ALB normally routes to healthy registered targets, but fails open to unhealthy targets when every registered target in a target group is unhealthy. Do not infer from an unhealthy result alone that no requests can be routed.''',
'''An unhealthy target label is not an unconditional no-traffic guarantee. The all-registered-targets-unhealthy case fails open. Keep that exception beside the normal healthy-target routing rule, and diagnose the supplied path using health reasons and backend settings.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f4', 'Preserve fails-open exception; do not introduce unsupported load-balancer algorithm or DNS behavior.')
add(59,'MEDIUM',
'''A log group needs the approved retention interval. Verify the configured policy without expecting expired events to disappear physically at the exact expiry moment.''',
'''Set the supplied interval with put-retention-policy and inspect retentionInDays on the intended log group. Compare that value with the requirement. Events that expire are marked for deletion, and physical deletion typically takes up to 72 hours. If an expired event remains visible immediately after expiry, keep that delay in the interpretation instead of assuming the retention setting is absent.''',
'''Retention expiry and physical deletion are not necessarily simultaneous. Logs are retained indefinitely by default; after a retention policy is set, expired events are marked for deletion, which typically takes up to 72 hours.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Preserve typically; do not promise a hard deletion deadline beyond the brief.')
add(60,'HIGH',
'''A database snapshot should support a verified restoration, not merely appear in a backup list. Follow the intended source snapshot into a separate restored instance and inspect its data marker.''',
'''Create the manual snapshot for the supplied DB instance and wait until the snapshot is available. Restore it with a new DB instance identifier and the supplied network settings. Wait for the restored instance to become available, then connect to that instance's endpoint and run the supplied recovery-marker query. Record the source, snapshot and restored identifier together. The restore creates a separate instance; it does not overwrite the original database.''',
'''An available snapshot is not the entire recovery verification. Restoration creates a separate instance, and the supplied query checks its marker after availability. Do not accidentally test the source endpoint and label that as restored-data evidence.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Use supplied network settings and query; do not author new SQL or database backup policy.')
add(61,'MEDIUM',
'''A reader needs approved access to a particular log group. Collection settings do not grant that reader access, so test authorization under the intended identities.''',
'''Grant the supplied reader the required read actions on the intended resources and remove unnecessary broader grants. Preserve the qualification that some operations require resource scope *. Test the intended read operations under the approved and unapproved principals. Record action, resource scope and identity with each result so the test demonstrates the requested access boundary rather than the collector's ability to write logs.''',
'''Collecting logs does not itself grant reader access. CloudWatch Logs authorization uses IAM policies, and some operations need resource scope *. Do not force a narrower unsupported scope just to make the policy look uniform.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied read actions and supported scopes; do not invent action-to-resource support rules.')
add(62,'HIGH',
'''A supplied image needs an ECS task definition with the approved runtime settings. Registration describes the task; running and inspection check the actual task instance.''',
'''Set the supplied containerDefinitions name, image, portMappings, cpu and memory appropriate to the exercise's launch type. Register the task definition and run it using the supplied cluster and network configuration. Inspect task status and container details, comparing image and runtime settings with the supplied specification. Keep the registered definition and observed task evidence connected instead of checking only that a task-definition revision exists.''',
'''A registered task definition is a specification, not proof that a task ran with the intended settings. Inspect task status and container details after the supplied run; do not generalize cpu/memory settings across launch types.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied launch-type settings; defer service desired-count and rollout operation.')
add(63,'HIGH',
'''An AWS request was denied even though one policy appears to allow it. Diagnose the specific request and applicable constraints before widening permissions. The principal, action, resource and context are the starting evidence.''',
'''Take the supplied denied request and write down the requesting identity, operation, target and context. Compare them with applicable policy statements and any available denial context. Locate an explicit deny, a missing applicable allow or an unsatisfied condition supported by the evidence. Do not stop after finding an allow in one identity policy: resource policies, session policies, permissions boundaries and organization policies can also constrain access. An applicable explicit deny takes precedence. Make the smallest approved request or policy correction and repeat the same operation as the test. Keep the before-and-after evidence specific to this request rather than claiming the corrected identity now has unrestricted access.''',
'''An access denial is not proof that the identity policy merely needs a broader Allow. Other policy constraints may apply, and explicit deny takes precedence. Use the request and denial evidence to choose a minimal approved correction, then retest that operation.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f2,f3,f4', 'Do not prescribe broad administrator grants or invent missing policies; follow supplied evidence.')

